# Eliminating a `Remote`.
#
# `readyOr` legitimises discarding the loading state, which is the one
# thing §5.2's second invariant exists to prevent. It is a legal
# elimination of the variant, so the rule is satisfied to the letter, and
# forbidding it here would only make every program write it out by hand —
# which is §14F.2's own argument for having a library at all. It is
# included and flagged, not defended.

function readyOr with result, fallback
    when result
        Loading
            give fallback
        Failed with error
            give fallback
        Ready with value
            give value

function isReady of result
    when result
        Loading
            give no
        Failed with error
            give no
        Ready with value
            give yes

# `Remote of Remote of T` flattened to `Remote of T`.
#
# What `flattenOption` is to #103, for #104, and the arms are why it has
# to be written out rather than shared: an outer `Loading` and an outer
# `Failed` are answers about the *outer* request and survive, while a
# `Ready` hands back whatever the inner one said.
function flattenRemote of result
    when result
        Loading
            give Loading
        Failed with error
            give Failed with error is error
        Ready with value
            give value

# Two `Remote of T`s as one.
#
# §17.7 recorded this as having no answer: "without records-in-the-library
# or tuples, `bothOf` has no return type to give", so nested `when` was
# the only form and §14G.1.6's three-arms-always rule made two remotes
# **nine arms written out** at every call site.
#
# `zip`'s comment in `list.zd` names the same wall and says it is one
# issue rather than two — a pair was what both were waiting for. `zip` got
# past it; this is the other half.
#
# The nine arms still exist. They are written here, once, instead of in
# every program that reads two server signals, which is what a library is
# for (§14F.2). A caller writes three.
#
# **Loading wins over Failed, and Failed over Ready.** A page showing
# "still loading" while one half has already failed would say the request
# is in flight when it is over; a page showing an error while the other
# half is still arriving would say it is over when it is not. Neither is
# wrong about the half it describes, and the caller cannot tell them
# apart from the outside, so the order is stated here rather than left to
# whichever `when` happens to be written first.
function bothOf with left, right
    when left
        Loading
            give Loading
        Failed with error
            give Failed with error is error
        Ready with first
            when right
                Loading
                    give Loading
                Failed with error
                    give Failed with error is error
                Ready with second
                    give Ready with value is (Pair with first is first, second is second)
